WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.720
There's a very interesting new poll from the
University of Southern California, which is

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:09.089
making headlines because Joe Biden is winning
by 11.

00:00:09.089 --> 00:00:10.150
I don't want to talk about that.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:14.860
I've already told you, you know, Joe, up 11
with a margin of error of three and a half

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.280
means maybe Joe is only up four.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.070
And if Trump picks up a point between now
and November, you could have the exact same

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:22.290
outcome as in 2016.

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:25.400
So I don't care about Biden up by 11:00.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:31.090
What I care about in this poll is that it
looks at nonvoters from 2016.

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:40.050
OK, in the United States, embarrassingly tragically,
disgustingly, only about 55 percent of eligible

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.170
voters vote.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:45.839
Forty five percent of those who could vote
did not vote in 2016.

00:00:45.839 --> 00:00:48.449
The turnout was 55 percent.

00:00:48.449 --> 00:00:53.929
The preliminary data from the University of
Southern California poll finds that among

00:00:53.929 --> 00:01:01.050
those who didn't vote in 2016 but planned
to vote this year, 52 percent plan to vote

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:06.300
for Joe Biden, while only 32 percent plan
to vote for Donald Trump.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.520
And another 16 percent say that they would
vote third party.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:17.370
Now, it's also notable that among those who
voted third party in 2016, they are also leaning

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:18.680
towards Biden.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:24.260
Forty one percent of those who were third
party in 2016 are going Biden, while only

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:29.070
30 percent of 2016 third party voters are
going for Donald Trump.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:30.820
That's huge.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:39.460
And of the people, when you combine that 71
percent of third party voters from last election

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:45.910
are not voting third party this time around,
which I think does go to the understood significance

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:47.070
of this election.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.030
But I want to focus on the nonvoters.

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:50.030
Okay.

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:55.060
2016 nonvoters who are going to vote are leaning
towards Biden.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:59.300
The obvious question is, how many people is
that right?

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:05.050
If there's a hundred thousand nonvoters from
last time who are going to vote.

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:12.390
That's very different than if there are 10
million nonvoters from 2016 who plan to vote,

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:15.370
if indeed 52 percent of them are going to
be voting for Joe Biden.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:20.620
And just as importantly, how many people are
there who did vote in 2016 and don't plan

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:21.620
to.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:23.780
This time around, no data on them.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:29.700
Another important question, but this is another
signal that our instinct from a year and a

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.350
half ago continues to be proven true.

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:37.610
We are way better off trying to just mobilize
people to vote than we are trying to argue

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:40.319
with Trump's to get them to change their minds.

00:02:40.319 --> 00:02:44.300
It's very clear that a majority of those who
didn't vote last time.

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:48.050
If we can get them to participate, that helps
Joe Biden.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:53.140
Just finding nonvoters from 2016 and getting
them to vote helps Joe Biden.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:57.150
If you can target it to people who are already
on the left but didn't vote.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.500
That's the win.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:04.990
Spending time arguing with Trump lists whose
brains have turned to mayonnaise is not a

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:06.830
good electoral strategy.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:09.780
And then we get to the problem of voter apathy
in the United States.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:16.390
There is no question, you know, when when
45 percent of the eligible voting public doesn't

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:17.390
vote.

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:23.490
There's no question that part of the problem
is we are not ingraining civic engagement

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:29.290
and the importance of voting from a young
age where if people are growing up in households

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:33.720
where, of course, you vote is the default.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:41.290
If kids are not hearing in school as early
as whenever we vote, that is what you do.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:45.720
If he if that was the situation, our turnout
would be far higher than 55 percent.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.480
That's one part of the problem.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:49.590
Our voting system is also part of the problem.

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:52.800
And we can't ignore that the system we have
dissuades voting.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.840
And if you look at other countries without
mandatory voting, with significantly higher

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.030
turnout, you see that sometimes they have
different election systems.

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:04.209
New Zealand has a mixed member proportional
system.

00:04:04.209 --> 00:04:06.800
Their turnout last election was almost 80
percent.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.610
Germany has a mixed member proportional system.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:11.489
Their turnout was 76 percent.

00:04:11.489 --> 00:04:17.419
We have a first past the post system that
perpetuates the two party duopoly and dissuades

00:04:17.419 --> 00:04:18.549
voting at all.

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:22.510
Now, many of you have called me during live
calls and you say, David, what the hell is

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:23.990
going on with third parties?

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:27.240
Why can't third parties get going in the United
States?

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:32.910
Part of it is the spoiler effect of the first
past, the post post system.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:34.860
And part of it also is campaign finance.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:36.610
So there are many problems here.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:43.430
I don't believe that the intent of the founding
fathers was to create a system that dissuades

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:47.790
participation, but it's what it's become in
the modern voting era.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:52.070
From the standpoint of this election, let's
target those who didn't vote last time.

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:53.070
It's a good idea.

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:54.130
The data backs it up.

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:57.220
We can't bank on nonvoters showing up.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:02.169
Some of the least reliable voters are people
who didn't vote last time, unfortunately.

00:05:02.169 --> 00:05:04.840
But the numbers are on our side.

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:10.870
Arguing with trombonists is a waste of time,
and the numbers show nonvoters from 2016 much

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:14.520
more likely to support Joe Biden than Donald
Trump.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:19.360
I think it's an interesting area to focus
part on, particularly in battleground states

00:05:19.360 --> 00:06:10.500
from here

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:31.990
on out.

